<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; line-height: 1.6; color: #333;">

    <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 15px 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
        <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Taking Care of Our Class – Lesson # 4</h1>
        <p style="margin: 5px 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Taking Care of Our Class</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> We Are Sweeping the Floors.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Create short descriptions of favorite characters from books. Interactive: Draft questions for guest speakers, including imaginary "guests" or "characters" of relevance.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can describe a character involved in classroom tasks (e.g., "My favorite character is Super Tidy. He always helps organize the desks and sweep the floor."). Can write simple questions for guest speakers (e.g., "How can we keep our classroom clean?"). Can share their character description with the class (e.g., "Super Tidy likes to clean. He likes to sweep the floor and arrange the desks.").</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 25px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large picture of a messy classroom and distributes a worksheet with the same picture. Students are instructed to label at least five items they see that need to be cleaned or organized (e.g., "dirty floor," "messy desk," "trash can"). They also write one short sentence describing what they think is happening (e.g., "The classroom is messy.").</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing simple sentences on the board using the present continuous and imperative forms, relating to classroom cleaning. For example, pointing to the messy floor in the picture, the teacher writes: "The floor is dirty. We are sweeping the floors. Sweep the floor, please!" The teacher explicitly uses vocabulary like "sweep," "arrange," "tidy," "desk," "chair," and adverbs "quickly," "carefully." The teacher points out the /r/ sound in "arrange" and the /s/ sound in "supplies."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the vocabulary and sentence structures.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "If the floor is dirty, what are we doing?" (Target answer: "We are sweeping.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "When I say 'Please arrange the chairs,' what do I want you to do?" (Target answer: "Put them in order.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows flashcards of "dustpan" and "mop," asking students to write the words.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt (a paragraph or two) about "Super Tidy," an imaginary classroom helper character, describing his daily routine. The text includes present continuous, imperatives, and modal "can" sentences, focusing on classroom cleaning vocabulary. (e.g., "Super Tidy is always busy. He is sweeping the floor quickly. He can wipe down the desks carefully. Please organize the books!")</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand and extract specific information from a short descriptive text related to classroom cleaning tasks and a character.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the text about "Super Tidy" individually.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question (written):</b> "Who is Super Tidy and what does he do?" (Students write a 1-2 sentence answer.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (written):</b> Students write 'T' or 'F' next to sentences based on the text. For example: "Super Tidy is sleeping. (F)" "He is cleaning the windows. (T)"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (written):</b> Students match a cleaning task to a cleaning supply, writing the pairs. (e.g., "sweeping" -> "mop," "wiping" -> "cloth").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing sentences using present continuous, imperatives, and modal "can" with target vocabulary (e.g., "sweep," "wipe," "arrange," "desk," "chair," "clean," "tidy").</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentence frames or cloze activities where students complete sentences using vocabulary words (nouns, verbs, adjectives) and correct grammar. E.g., "The teacher is __________ the whiteboard with a __________. (wipe, cleaner)" or "__________ the books on the shelf, __________! (arrange, please)". Students write their answers.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Transformation):</b> Students are given simple sentences and asked to rewrite them using a different target structure or vocabulary word. E.g., "Clean the window." (Rewrite using 'can' and 'please': "Can you please clean the window?") or "The desks are not tidy." (Rewrite using 'arrange': "Let's arrange the desks."). Students write the new sentences.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Instruction Writing):</b> In pairs, students take turns writing a simple instruction for their partner related to cleaning the classroom, using an imperative. The partner then writes a response offering help using "Can I help you...?" For example: Partner A writes: "Please wipe the table." Partner B writes: "Can I help you wipe the table?" They switch roles and repeat three times.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual written product (character description and drafted questions).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are instructed to imagine their own "Classroom Cleaning Character" (like "Super Tidy").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They will write a short description (3-4 sentences) of their character, focusing on what the character does to keep the classroom clean. They must use at least two verbs in the present continuous (e.g., "He is sweeping," "She is organizing"), one imperative (e.g., "Clean up!"), and one "can" sentence (e.g., "He can carry a big bucket."). They should include 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., "mop," "desk," "tidy," "quickly").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After the description, students will draft two simple questions they would ask their character about keeping the classroom clean, using "How can we...?" or "What do you...?" (e.g., "How can we keep the windows clean?" "What is your favorite cleaning supply?").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students then share their character descriptions and questions with a partner, reading them aloud. (Addresses "Can share their character description with the class" learning outcome).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their written character description and drafted questions.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer Check):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Completeness:</b> Is the character description clear? Are there 2 questions?</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Are at least 3-4 vocabulary words from the theme used correctly?</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Are present continuous, imperatives, and "can" used correctly? (e.g., subject-verb agreement, verb forms)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Overall Writing Quality:</b> Is it easy to read and understand for A1.3 level?</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts on a small slip of paper:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. One thing I learned to write today is...</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. One word or sentence I still need to practice writing is...</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. How did writing about a cleaning character help me practice new words?</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. What part of keeping our classroom clean is most important to my character?</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in terms of student engagement and writing output? Did students successfully integrate the target grammar and vocabulary in their descriptions and questions? What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with sentence formation or vocabulary recall in writing? (e.g., more sentence frames, mini-whiteboard practice).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their character descriptions and questions to create a "Classroom Cleaning Hero Poster" and present their character, integrating their written work into a final project.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[3 min]</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>